package android.hardware.tetheroffload.control@1.1;

import @1.0::IOffloadControl;

/**
 * Interface used to control the lifecycle of tethering offload
 */
interface IOffloadControl extends @1.0::IOffloadControl {
  /**
   * Instruct hardware to send a callback after quota bytes have been transferred in either
   * direction on this upstream interface.
   *
   * The quota must be applied to all traffic on the given upstream interface. This includes
   * hardware forwarded traffic, software forwarded traffic, and AP-originated traffic. IPv4 and
   * IPv6 traffic both count towards the same quota. IP headers are included in the byte count
   * quota, but, link-layer headers are not.
   *
   * This API may only be called while offload is occurring on this upstream.  The hardware
   * management process is not expected to cache the value and apply the quota once offload is
   * started.  This cache is not expected, because the quota value would likely become stale over
   * time and would not reflect any new traffic that has occurred.
   *
   * The quota must replace any previous call with any quota specified on the same interface. It
   * may be interpreted as "tell me when <quota> bytes have been transferred (in either direction)
   * on <upstream>, starting now and counting from zero."
   *
   * Once the quota is reached, the callback registered in initOffload must be called with
   * {@code OFFLOAD_WARNING_REACHED} to indicate this event. Once the event fires for this upstream,
   * no further event will be fired for this upstream unless this method is called again with the
   * same interface. Note that it is no need to call initOffload again to resume offload if
   * stopOffload was not called by the client. A caller may call both {@code setDataWarning} and
   * {@code setDataLimit}, and both quota must be able to operate independently.
   *
   * @param upstream Upstream interface name that quota must apply to.
   * @param quota The quota defined as the number of bytes, starting from zero and counting from
   *              now.
   *
   * @return success true if quota is applied, false otherwise
   * @return errMsg a human readable string if error has occurred.
   */
  setDataWarning(string upstream, uint64_t quota) generates (bool success, string errMsg);
};
